1
00:00:01,800 --> 00:00:05,881
Outside the walls of Troy
stands a giant wooden horse.

2
00:00:06,181 --> 00:00:11,187
The Greeks, it seems, have sailed
away and left it behind as a gift.

3
00:00:11,487 --> 00:00:14,773
But inside, soldiers are
waiting for the night.

4
00:00:15,073 --> 00:00:18,374
It is one of the most
famous stories ever told,

5
00:00:18,374 --> 00:00:21,675
and many people remember
it from Homer's Iliad.

6
00:00:21,975 --> 00:00:25,569
Yet the Iliad ends before
the horse is ever built.

7
00:00:25,869 --> 00:00:29,199
So where does the story
of the Trojan horse come from?

8
00:00:32,300 --> 00:00:37,418
In this episode, we follow the horse
from the last line of the Iliad,

9
00:00:37,418 --> 00:00:42,311
to a song in the Odyssey, to poems
now mostly lost, and to a Roman

10
00:00:42,311 --> 00:00:46,451
poet who gave the story one
of its most famous tellings.

11
00:00:51,867 --> 00:00:55,909
The Iliad is one of the oldest
works of Greek literature.

12
00:00:56,209 --> 00:01:00,414
It tells of only a short time
in the tenth year of the Trojan

13
00:01:00,414 --> 00:01:03,917
War, beginning with the anger
of the hero Achilles.

14
00:01:04,217 --> 00:01:07,993
It ends not with the fall
of Troy, but with a funeral.

15
00:01:08,293 --> 00:01:11,715
In one old translation,
its final line reads:

16
00:01:11,715 --> 00:01:15,682
"Thus held they funeral
for Hector tamer of horses."

17
00:01:16,667 --> 00:01:21,145
The Iliad does not tell of a wooden
horse, or of the city's fall.

18
00:01:21,445 --> 00:01:25,529
Even the death of Achilles
is foretold, but never shown.

19
00:01:25,829 --> 00:01:28,997
The horse, the trick, the burning of Troy:

20
00:01:28,997 --> 00:01:32,317
all of that lies beyond
the poem's last page.

21
00:01:34,133 --> 00:01:38,335
The horse does appear in Homer's
other great poem, the Odyssey.

22
00:01:38,635 --> 00:01:42,235
Far from home, Odysseus
sits at a feast and asks

23
00:01:42,235 --> 00:01:46,370
the blind singer Demodocus
to sing of the wooden horse,

24
00:01:46,370 --> 00:01:50,506
which Epeius built with the help
of the goddess Athena.

25
00:01:50,806 --> 00:01:55,680
In the song, the Trojans argue over
what to do with it: break it open,

26
00:01:55,680 --> 00:02:00,413
throw it down onto the rocks, or keep
it as an offering to the gods.

27
00:02:02,600 --> 00:02:06,938
Earlier in the poem, King Menelaus
remembers how Helen walked

28
00:02:06,938 --> 00:02:11,203
around the horse, calling out
the names of the Greek leaders

29
00:02:11,203 --> 00:02:15,107
hidden inside, and imitating
the voices of their wives.

30
00:02:15,407 --> 00:02:18,726
Odysseus held the men back,
and covered one man's

31
00:02:18,726 --> 00:02:22,115
mouth with his hands, so
that no one would answer.

32
00:02:23,033 --> 00:02:27,583
And in the land of the dead,
Odysseus tells the ghost of Achilles

33
00:02:27,583 --> 00:02:32,205
that his son was among the men
inside, and never once turned pale.

34
00:02:32,505 --> 00:02:37,024
In Homer, the horse is a memory,
told in a few dozen lines.

35
00:02:37,324 --> 00:02:41,024
None of these passages says
how many men were inside.

36
00:02:43,233 --> 00:02:46,633
Other early Greek poems
told the end of the war,

37
00:02:46,633 --> 00:02:49,887
such as the Little Iliad
and the Sack of Troy.

38
00:02:50,187 --> 00:02:52,247
They do not survive in full.

39
00:02:52,547 --> 00:02:56,910
We know them mainly through short
summaries written centuries later.

40
00:02:57,210 --> 00:03:01,673
Yet those summaries already have
the horse taken into the city

41
00:03:01,673 --> 00:03:06,574
through a broken wall, the Trojan
priest Laocoön killed by serpents,

42
00:03:06,574 --> 00:03:10,305
and Sinon, a Greek who helps
to deceive the Trojans.

43
00:03:11,833 --> 00:03:14,315
Artists knew the story early, too.

44
00:03:14,615 --> 00:03:19,157
A large clay jar found
on the Greek island of Mykonos,

45
00:03:19,157 --> 00:03:24,128
made around 670 BC, shows
a horse on wheels, with the faces

46
00:03:24,128 --> 00:03:28,585
of warriors looking out
of small windows in its side.

47
00:03:28,885 --> 00:03:32,896
It is one of the oldest known
pictures of the Trojan horse.

48
00:03:33,800 --> 00:03:36,937
The version most of us
know comes from Rome.

49
00:03:37,237 --> 00:03:41,683
About two thousand years ago,
the poet Virgil wrote the Aeneid.

50
00:03:41,983 --> 00:03:47,635
In its second book, the Trojan hero Aeneas
tells a queen of his city's last night.

51
00:03:47,935 --> 00:03:53,368
The priest Laocoön warns, in one old
translation, "I fear the Grecians

52
00:03:53,368 --> 00:03:58,171
even when they offer gifts,"
and hurls his spear at the horse.

53
00:03:58,471 --> 00:04:00,787
Sinon tells a convincing lie.

54
00:04:01,087 --> 00:04:03,041
Serpents come from the sea.

55
00:04:03,341 --> 00:04:07,703
And the Trojans open their own
walls to pull the horse inside.

56
00:04:08,900 --> 00:04:12,118
Virgil wrote many centuries
after Homer's poems,

57
00:04:12,118 --> 00:04:15,609
and he told the story
from the side of the defeated.

58
00:04:15,909 --> 00:04:19,671
He did not invent the horse,
the serpents, or Sinon.

59
00:04:19,971 --> 00:04:24,231
But many of the scenes we picture
today, the warning, the lie,

60
00:04:24,231 --> 00:04:28,421
the opened wall, come to us most
vividly through his telling.

61
00:04:35,067 --> 00:04:39,269
At least one ancient writer
offered another explanation.

62
00:04:39,569 --> 00:04:43,455
In the second century AD,
the travel writer Pausanias

63
00:04:43,455 --> 00:04:47,490
saw a bronze statue in Athens,
called the Wooden Horse.

64
00:04:47,790 --> 00:04:52,313
He wrote that anyone who did not
think the Trojans utterly foolish

65
00:04:52,313 --> 00:04:57,183
knew the horse had really been
a machine for breaking through the wall.

66
00:04:57,483 --> 00:04:59,545
And how many men were inside?

67
00:04:59,845 --> 00:05:02,723
One later handbook gives fifty, but adds

68
00:05:02,723 --> 00:05:06,681
that the author of the Little
Iliad said three thousand.

69
00:05:07,600 --> 00:05:10,461
So, is the Trojan horse in the Iliad?

70
00:05:10,761 --> 00:05:12,161
No.

71
00:05:12,211 --> 00:05:17,313
It appears briefly in the Odyssey,
grew in poems now mostly lost

72
00:05:17,313 --> 00:05:22,416
and in pictures, and survives
in rich detail in Virgil's Aeneid.

73
00:05:22,716 --> 00:05:26,384
Today, even computer
programs that look useful but

74
00:05:26,384 --> 00:05:29,902
hide something harmful
are called Trojan horses.

75
00:05:30,202 --> 00:05:33,542
The gift is still remembered,
and still not trusted.

76
00:05:36,500 --> 00:05:38,827
Every story has an origin.

77
00:05:39,127 --> 00:05:42,615
Sometimes, it begins
with a song sung at a feast.

78
00:05:44,300 --> 00:05:49,459
This video is based on historical
texts and public museum collections.

79
00:05:49,759 --> 00:05:52,479
The narration uses synthetic speech.

80
00:05:52,779 --> 00:05:56,725
Sources and terms of use are
listed in the description.

81
00:05:57,025 --> 00:05:59,794
If you enjoyed this story, please like

82
00:05:59,794 --> 00:06:03,146
the video and subscribe
for more quiet origins.

83
00:06:03,446 --> 00:06:05,219
Thank you for watching.
